    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L07-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: What’s the Equation?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let’s say we want a polynomial function \\(Z\\) that satisfies \\(Z(x)=0\\) when \\(x\\) is -1, 2, or 4. We know that one way to write a polynomial expression is as a product of linear factors. We could write a possible expression for \\(Z(x)\\)…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L07-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: What’s the Equation?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "It turns out that there are many possible expressions for \\(Z(x)\\). Using linear factors, one possibility is \\(Z(x)=(x+1)(x-2)(x-4)\\). Another possibility is \\(Z(x)=2(x+1)(x-2)(x-4)\\), since the 2 (or any other rational number) does not…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L07-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "7.3: What’s the Equation?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "To check that these expressions match what we know about \\(Z\\), we can test the three values -1, 2, and 4 to make sure that \\(Z(x)\\) is 0 for those values. Alternatively, we can graph both possible versions of \\(Z\\) and see that the graphs…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l07.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
